                            display_name: {
                                english: "Nervous System & Reflex Actions",
                                hindi: "Nervous System और Reflex Actions"
                            },
                            description: {
                                english: "1. Control and coordination in animals: Provided by nervous and muscular tissues.\n\n2. Receptors: Specialised tips of nerve cells detect information (e.g., gustatory receptors detect taste, olfactory receptors detect smell). Located in sense organs.\n\n3. Neuron (nerve cell): Structural and functional unit of nervous system. Parts: Dendrite (acquires information), Cell body (contains nucleus), Axon (carries electrical impulse), Nerve ending (converts impulse to chemical signal).\n\n4. Synapse: Gap between two neurons. Electrical impulse sets off release of chemicals (neurotransmitters) which cross synapse and start similar impulse in next neuron.\n\n5. Neuromuscular junction: Similar synapse between neuron and muscle cell.\n\n6. Reflex action: Sudden action in response to something in the environment, without thinking. E.g., pulling hand back from flame.\n\n7. Reflex arc: Connection between input nerve (detects heat) and output nerve (moves muscle) formed in spinal cord. Information also goes to brain. Allows quick response.\n\n8. Human brain: Fore-brain (main thinking part; regions for hearing, smell, sight, sensory interpretation, motor control, hunger centre), Mid-brain and Hind-brain (involuntary actions: blood pressure, salivation, vomiting controlled by medulla; cerebellum responsible for precision of voluntary actions, posture, balance).\n\n9. Protection: Brain inside bony box (cranium), contained in fluid-filled balloon (shock absorption). Spinal cord protected by vertebral column (backbone).\n\n10. Central nervous system (CNS): Brain + spinal cord. Peripheral nervous system (PNS): Cranial nerves (from brain) + spinal nerves (from spinal cord).\n\n11. Muscles: Move by changing shape (shorten). Special proteins change shape and arrangement in response to nervous electrical impulses.",
                                hindi: "1. Animals में control और coordination: Nervous और muscular tissues से provided.\n\n2. Receptors: Nerve cells के specialised tips information detect करते हैं (जैसे gustatory receptors taste detect, olfactory receptors smell detect). Sense organs में located.\n\n3. Neuron (nerve cell): Nervous system की structural और functional unit. Parts: Dendrite (information acquire), Cell body (nucleus contain), Axon (electrical impulse carry), Nerve ending (impulse को chemical signal में convert).\n\n4. Synapse: दो neurons के बीच gap. Electrical impulse chemicals (neurotransmitters) का release set off करता है जो synapse cross करके next neuron में similar impulse start करते हैं.\n\n5. Neuromuscular junction: Neuron और muscle cell के बीच similar synapse.\n\n6. Reflex action: Environment में किसी चीज़ के response में sudden action, बिना सोचे. जैसे flame से hand पीछे खींचना.\n\n7. Reflex arc: Input nerve (heat detect) और output nerve (muscle move) के बीच connection spinal cord में formed. Information brain तक भी जाती है. Quick response allow करता है.\n\n8. Human brain: Fore-brain (main thinking part; hearing, smell, sight, sensory interpretation, motor control, hunger centre के regions), Mid-brain और Hind-brain (involuntary actions: blood pressure, salivation, vomiting medulla से controlled; cerebellum voluntary actions की precision, posture, balance के लिए responsible).\n\n9. Protection: Brain bony box (cranium) के अंदर, fluid-filled balloon में contained (shock absorption). Spinal cord vertebral column (backbone) से protected.\n\n10. Central nervous system (CNS): Brain + spinal cord. Peripheral nervous system (PNS): Cranial nerves (brain से) + spinal nerves (spinal cord से).\n\n11. Muscles: Shape change करके move (shorten). Special proteins nervous electrical impulses के response में shape और arrangement change करते हैं.\nKeywords: receptors, neuron, dendrite, axon, synapse, neurotransmitter, reflex action, reflex arc, spinal cord, fore-brain, mid-brain, hind-brain, medulla, cerebellum, CNS, PNS"
                            },
                            diagrams: null
