                            id: "sci10_c13",
                            display_name: {
                                english: "Current, Potential Difference, Ohm's Law & Resistance",
                                hindi: "Current, Potential Difference, Ohm's Law और Resistance"
                            },
                            description: {
                                english: "1. Electric current (I): Rate of flow of electric charges. I = Q/t. SI unit: ampere (A). 1 A = 1 C/s.\n\n2. Ammeter: Measures current. Connected in series.\n\n3. Electric potential difference (V): Work done to move a unit charge from one point to another. V = W/Q. SI unit: volt (V). 1 V = 1 J/C.\n\n4. Voltmeter: Measures potential difference. Connected in parallel.\n\n5. Ohm's law: Potential difference across ends of a metallic wire is directly proportional to current, provided temperature remains same. V ∝ I, V/I = constant = R.\n\n6. Resistance (R): Property of conductor to resist flow of charges. SI unit: ohm (Ω). R = V/I. 1 Ω = 1 V / 1 A.\n\n7. Variable resistance (rheostat): Regulates current without changing voltage source.\n\n8. Factors affecting resistance: R ∝ l (length), R ∝ 1/A (area of cross-section), depends on material. R = ρl/A.\n\n9. Resistivity (ρ): Characteristic property of material. SI unit: Ω m. Metals and alloys have low resistivity (10⁻⁸ to 10⁻⁶ Ω m). Insulators have high resistivity (10¹² to 10¹⁷ Ω m).\n\n10. Series combination: Current same through each resistor. Total potential difference = sum of individual potential differences. Equivalent resistance Rs = R1 + R2 + R3.\n\n11. Parallel combination: Potential difference same across each resistor. Total current = sum of individual currents. 1/Rp = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3.\n\n12. Example 11.1: I = 0.5 A, t = 600 s → Q = It = 300 C.\n\n13. Example 11.2: Q = 2 C, V = 12 V → W = VQ = 24 J.\n\n14. Example 11.4: V = 60 V, I = 4 A → R = 15 Ω. When V = 120 V, I = 8 A.\n\n15. Example 11.5: R = 26 Ω, d = 0.3 mm, l = 1 m → ρ = 1.84 × 10⁻⁶ Ω m (manganese).\n\n16. Example 11.6: R1 = 4 Ω (l, A). R2 for l/2, 2A → R2 = 1 Ω.",
                                hindi: "1. Electric current (I): Electric charges के flow की rate. I = Q/t. SI unit: ampere (A). 1 A = 1 C/s.\n\n2. Ammeter: Current measure करता है. Series में connected.\n\n3. Electric potential difference (V): एक point से दूसरे point तक unit charge move करने में work done. V = W/Q. SI unit: volt (V). 1 V = 1 J/C.\n\n4. Voltmeter: Potential difference measure करता है. Parallel में connected.\n\n5. Ohm's law: Metallic wire के ends के across potential difference current के directly proportional है, बशर्ते temperature same रहे. V ∝ I, V/I = constant = R.\n\n6. Resistance (R): Charges के flow को resist करने का conductor का property. SI unit: ohm (Ω). R = V/I. 1 Ω = 1 V / 1 A.\n\n7. Variable resistance (rheostat): Voltage source बदले बिना current regulate करता है.\n\n8. Resistance को affect करने वाले factors: R ∝ l (length), R ∝ 1/A (area of cross-section), material पर depend. R = ρl/A.\n\n9. Resistivity (ρ): Material का characteristic property. SI unit: Ω m. Metals और alloys की low resistivity (10⁻⁸ to 10⁻⁶ Ω m). Insulators की high resistivity (10¹² to 10¹⁷ Ω m).\n\n10. Series combination: प्रत्येक resistor में current same. Total potential difference = individual potential differences का sum. Equivalent resistance Rs = R1 + R2 + R3.\n\n11. Parallel combination: प्रत्येक resistor के across potential difference same. Total current = individual currents का sum. 1/Rp = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3.\n\n12. Example 11.1: I = 0.5 A, t = 600 s → Q = It = 300 C.\n\n13. Example 11.2: Q = 2 C, V = 12 V → W = VQ = 24 J.\n\n14. Example 11.4: V = 60 V, I = 4 A → R = 15 Ω. जब V = 120 V, I = 8 A.\n\n15. Example 11.5: R = 26 Ω, d = 0.3 mm, l = 1 m → ρ = 1.84 × 10⁻⁶ Ω m (manganese).\n\n16. Example 11.6: R1 = 4 Ω (l, A). l/2, 2A के लिए R2 → R2 = 1 Ω.\nKeywords: electric current, ampere, potential difference, volt, ohm's law, resistance, ohm, rheostat, resistivity, series combination, parallel combination"
                            },
                            diagrams: null
